<span class="kicker">2026-08-29 / RIDGEWELL GUIDES</span><h1>How to Put a Paver Walkway Over a Concrete Walkway</h1>
</div>
</section>
<section class="section article-section"><div class="wrap editorial-layout"><article class="prose blog-content"><p>An existing concrete walkway that’s structurally sound but outdated or unattractive doesn’t necessarily need to be torn out before installing pavers. In many cases, pavers can be installed directly over concrete, using the existing slab as a stable base rather than starting excavation from scratch. This can save real time and cost compared to full removal and rebuild, but it only works when the concrete is actually in suitable condition, and the installation method, mortar-set or sand-set, needs to match your specific situation.</p>
<p>At Ridgewell Landscape and Design, we evaluate concrete-over-concrete paver projects across the Denver area, including Broomfield, Parker, and the surrounding communities, on a regular basis, and getting the assessment and method choice right upfront prevents a lot of problems down the line. This guide covers how to inspect your existing concrete, compares mortar-set versus sand-set installation methods, and addresses the height and clearance issues that come with building on top of an existing surface.</p>
<h2><b>Mortar-Set Versus Sand-Set: The Two Main Methods</b></h2>
<table><tbody><tr><td><b>Factor</b></td><td><b>Mortar-Set</b></td><td><b>Sand-Set</b></td></tr><tr><td>How pavers attach</td><td>Bonded directly to concrete with mortar or adhesive</td><td>Set into a sand bed, not directly bonded</td></tr><tr><td>Requires a rigid, crack-free base</td><td>Yes, essential</td><td>More forgiving of minor imperfections</td></tr><tr><td>Repair approach</td><td>Difficult, often requires breaking bond</td><td>Individual pavers can be lifted and reset</td></tr><tr><td>Height added</td><td>Lower, thinner overall buildup</td><td>Higher, needs a sand bed plus paver thickness</td></tr><tr><td>Best for</td><td>Excellent condition concrete, minimal height concerns</td><td>Concrete with minor flaws, more flexibility needed</td></tr><tr><td>Freeze-thaw performance</td><td>Can be more prone to cracking if concrete moves</td><td>Better tolerance for minor movement</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Mortar-set installation bonds pavers directly to the existing concrete surface, essentially treating the concrete as a rigid foundation similar to how tile is installed indoors. Sand-set installation uses a thin sand or specialized bedding layer between the concrete and the pavers, allowing slightly more flexibility and making individual paver repair more realistic down the line.</p>
<h2><b>Assessing Your Existing Concrete Before Starting</b></h2>
<h3><b>Checking for Cracks</b></h3>
<p>Minor surface cracks, hairline cracks that haven’t shifted or widened, are generally acceptable for either method, though they’re worth noting since they can sometimes telegraph through a mortar-set installation over time. Larger, wider cracks, especially any showing vertical displacement where one side sits higher than the other, are a bigger concern, since they suggest ongoing movement that will likely continue affecting whatever is built on top.</p>
<h3><b>Checking for Heaving or Settling</b></h3>
<p>Walk the length of the concrete looking and feeling for any areas that rise or dip noticeably compared to the surrounding surface. Heaving, often caused by tree roots or frost activity, and settling, often caused by erosion or poor original base preparation, both indicate the concrete isn’t sitting on stable, consistent ground, which raises the risk of that instability showing up again in a new surface built on top.</p>
<h3><b>Assessing Drainage</b></h3>
<p>Confirm that water isn’t pooling on the existing concrete after rain. Standing water indicates a drainage or grading issue with the slab itself, one that installing pavers on top won’t resolve and may actually worsen if the added height changes how water moves around the area.</p>
<h3><b>Structural Soundness Overall</b></h3>
<p>If the concrete is structurally sound, no major cracking, minimal to no heaving or settling, and reasonable drainage, it’s generally a good candidate for either method. If it’s showing multiple significant issues, it may be more cost-effective in the long run to remove the existing concrete and build a proper paver walkway from a fully excavated base instead. Our general guide on<a href="/blog/how-to-build-a-paver-walkway/"> how to build a paver walkway</a> covers that full-removal approach if your concrete assessment points in that direction.</p>
<h2><b>When Mortar-Set Makes Sense</b></h2>
<p>Mortar-set installation works best on concrete in excellent condition, minimal cracking, no heaving, good drainage, since the method relies on the concrete providing a genuinely stable, unmoving foundation. It also tends to be the better choice when height is a significant concern, since it adds less overall buildup than a sand-set approach requiring a full bedding layer.</p>
<h2><b>When Sand-Set Makes Sense</b></h2>
<p>Sand-set installation is generally the more forgiving choice, better suited to concrete with minor imperfections, some hairline cracking, or slight unevenness, since the sand layer can absorb small irregularities that would otherwise telegraph through a rigid mortar bond. It also allows for more realistic future repairs, since individual pavers can be lifted and reset without disturbing the surrounding installation the way a mortar-bonded paver typically requires.</p>
<h2><b>What You’ll Need</b></h2>
<p><b>For Mortar-Set Installation</b></p>
<ul><li>Pavers rated for mortar-set application</li><li>Bonding mortar or a paver-specific adhesive</li><li>Notched trowel for spreading mortar</li><li>Rubber mallet</li><li>Level</li><li>Grout or polymeric sand for joints, depending on paver spacing</li></ul>
<p><b>For Sand-Set Installation</b></p>
<ul><li>Pavers</li><li>Bedding sand or a specialized thin-set sand product designed for over-concrete applications</li><li>Edge restraints, secured into the concrete itself rather than into soil</li><li>Rubber mallet</li><li>Level</li><li>Polymeric sand for joints</li></ul>
<h2><b>Step by Step: Mortar-Set Installation Over Concrete</b></h2>
<h3><b>Step 1: Clean and Prep the Concrete Surface</b></h3>
<p>Thoroughly clean the existing concrete, removing any dirt, debris, oil stains, or loose material, since mortar bonds poorly to a dirty or contaminated surface.</p>
<h3><b>Step 2: Repair Minor Cracks First</b></h3>
<p>Fill and seal any hairline cracks before installation, reducing the chance they telegraph through the new surface over time.</p>
<h3><b>Step 3: Apply Bonding Mortar or Adhesive</b></h3>
<p>Spread mortar or paver adhesive using a notched trowel, working in manageable sections rather than covering the entire area at once, since mortar can begin setting before you’re ready to place pavers over it.</p>
<h3><b>Step 4: Set Pavers Into the Mortar</b></h3>
<p>Place each paver into the wet mortar, tapping gently with a rubber mallet to seat it and checking level frequently as you work.</p>
<h3><b>Step 5: Allow Proper Curing Time</b></h3>
<p>Let the mortar cure fully according to the product’s specifications before foot traffic or jointing work begins, typically at least 24 to 48 hours depending on conditions.</p>
<h3><b>Step 6: Fill Joints</b></h3>
<p>Once cured, fill joints with grout or polymeric sand depending on your paver spacing and design.</p>

