<h2><b>Step by Step: Sand-Set Installation Over Concrete</b></h2>
<h3><b>Step 1: Clean the Concrete Surface</b></h3>
<p>Clean thoroughly, removing debris and any loose material that could interfere with the sand bed sitting evenly across the surface.</p>
<h3><b>Step 2: Install Edge Restraints Anchored Into the Concrete</b></h3>
<p>Since there’s no soil to anchor edge restraints into, they need to be secured directly into the concrete using appropriate concrete anchors, establishing the boundary for your sand bed and pavers.</p>
<h3><b>Step 3: Add and Screed a Bedding Layer</b></h3>
<p>Add a thin bedding layer, either standard coarse sand or a specialized thin-set product designed for over-concrete applications, and screed it level across the surface.</p>
<h3><b>Step 4: Lay the Pavers</b></h3>
<p>Place pavers into the sand bed following your chosen pattern, tapping into position with a rubber mallet and checking level regularly. Our guide on<a href="/blog/how-to-lay-a-paver-walkway/"> how to lay a paver walkway</a> covers pattern and technique fundamentals that apply here as well.</p>
<h3><b>Step 5: Fill Joints With Polymeric Sand</b></h3>
<p>Sweep polymeric sand into the joints, mist to activate, and allow it to cure according to product instructions.</p>
<h2><b>Checking Height and Clearance Before You Commit</b></h2>
<h3><b>Why Added Height Matters</b></h3>
<p>Both methods add height above the existing concrete surface, generally an inch or more depending on paver thickness and installation method, which needs to be checked against nearby doorways, thresholds, and any adjoining hardscape before starting.</p>
<h3><b>Checking Door Clearance</b></h3>
<p>If the walkway leads to an exterior door, measure the current clearance between the concrete surface and the bottom of the door before installation. Adding paver height on top of the concrete can reduce that clearance meaningfully, and in some cases create a tripping hazard or interfere with the door’s swing if not accounted for ahead of time.</p>
<h3><b>Checking Transitions to Adjacent Surfaces</b></h3>
<p>Where the new paver surface meets an adjoining driveway, patio, or lawn area, confirm the height difference created by the added buildup doesn’t create an awkward or unsafe step. In some cases, a small transition detail, a beveled edge or a short connecting ramp, is needed to smooth out the height change where the new surface meets existing grade.</p>
<h2><b>Cost Considerations for Building Over Existing Concrete</b></h2>
<p>Building over existing concrete generally costs less than full removal and new base installation, since it skips the excavation and base compaction stages entirely. That said, the added labor for mortar work or specialized thin-set sand installation, along with the concrete assessment and any necessary crack repair, still represents a meaningful cost, and it’s worth getting a specific quote for this approach rather than assuming it will automatically be the cheaper option in every case. Our<a href="/blog/how-much-does-a-paver-walkway-cost/"> paver walkway cost guide</a> covers general new-construction pricing that can serve as a useful comparison point.</p>
<h2><b>Common Mistakes When Installing Pavers Over Concrete</b></h2>
<ul><li>Skipping a thorough condition assessment and building over concrete with significant cracking or heaving</li><li>Choosing mortar-set on concrete that isn’t in excellent condition, risking cracks telegraphing through</li><li>Not checking door and threshold clearance before finalizing the installation plan</li><li>Anchoring edge restraints improperly for a sand-set installation over a hard concrete surface</li><li>Ignoring existing drainage issues that installing pavers on top won’t resolve</li></ul>
<h2><b>When to Remove the Concrete Instead</b></h2>
<p>If your assessment reveals significant cracking, heaving, or drainage problems, building over that concrete generally isn’t the more cost-effective long-term choice, even though it might seem like the faster, cheaper option upfront. In these cases, removing the concrete and building a proper paver walkway from a fully excavated and compacted base tends to produce a more durable, longer-lasting result.</p>
<p>With over 15 years of assessing and building both over-concrete and full-removal paver walkway projects across the Denver area, our team at Ridgewell Landscape and Design can help you determine which approach makes the most sense for your specific existing concrete. Explore our<a href="/services/paver-patios/"> paver patio services</a> to see examples of completed projects using both methods.</p>

<h2><b>Frequently Asked Questions</b></h2>
<h3><b>Can I put pavers directly over an existing concrete walkway?</b></h3>
<p>Yes, in many cases, as long as the concrete is in reasonably good condition without significant cracking, heaving, or drainage issues.</p>
<h3><b>What’s the difference between mortar-set and sand-set installation over concrete?</b></h3>
<p>Mortar-set bonds pavers directly to the concrete using mortar or adhesive, while sand-set uses a sand bedding layer between the concrete and pavers, offering more flexibility and easier future repairs.</p>
<h3><b>How much height does installing pavers over concrete add?</b></h3>
<p>Generally an inch or more, depending on paver thickness and installation method, which should be checked against door clearances and adjoining surfaces before starting.</p>
<h3><b>Is it cheaper to build over existing concrete than to remove it?</b></h3>
<p>Often yes, since it skips excavation and base compaction, though the specific savings depend on the condition of the concrete and the installation method chosen.</p>
<h3><b>What concrete condition issues mean I shouldn’t build over it?</b></h3>
<p>Significant cracking with vertical displacement, noticeable heaving or settling, and persistent drainage problems all suggest removal and a full rebuild will produce a more durable result than building on top.</p>
<h3><b>Can individual pavers be repaired if they’re mortar-set over concrete?</b></h3>
<p>Not as easily as with a sand-set installation. Mortar-set pavers are more difficult to remove and reset individually without disturbing the surrounding bond.</p>

<h2><b>Ready to Build Over Your Existing Concrete Walkway</b></h2>
<p>Whether your concrete is a good candidate for building over or needs to come out entirely, getting an accurate assessment upfront saves you from a costly mistake either way.</p>
<p>Ridgewell Landscape and Design has been assessing and building paver walkways over both new and existing surfaces across Denver, Broomfield, Parker, and the surrounding communities for over 15 years. If you have an existing concrete walkway you’re considering building over,<a href="/contact/"> reach out for a free quote and consultation</a>.</p>

